nding why  people marry when people marry and the  quality of their marriage so I backed  into it and how do how does the paper he  present it in Hong Kong fit into this  larger project um what I did in Hong I  should step back last year when I was  working in Hong Kong  it became fascinating to me in the ways  in which marriage and sexual practices  in Hong Kong were looking more and more  on look what I was seeing on the other  side of the border and then when I  looked at other materials I realized  there was convergence between the Taiwan  Hong Kong and PRC experience so I put  together a group of people to  investigate what I call sexuality in  marriage and cosmopolitan China and the  paper I wrote in July is just one piece  of that comparison across the three  countries and my focus is particularly  on the legal aspect that is how the  States role visa vie marriage how  they've written the marriage law how  they've policed divorce in particular  reflects larger patterns of state  control over people's private lives and  what are some of the fundamental changes  in marriage that you're seeing in this  time period that you're researching oh  the time period is a little flexible  that is the paper really talks about the  last 10 years but the larger one goes  back 30 years in other words it goes  back to 1980 approximately when we think  of China's market reforms beginning it  also was the year in which they passed a  new marriage law and the marriage law  was sometimes seen by some people as a  divorce law because it made it much  easier for people to if couples agreed  to then simply file for divorce they  really didn't have to go through a court  procedure if both agreed and there was  no conflict  so it's over this 30 year period that I  that I'm really looking at there are  five things that are really remarkable  about 1980 to the present and first  comes from the affluence we all know  that that china has moved from being a  low income actually very impoverished  society to being lower middle income and  now more billionaires in China than any  place else in the world so you also have  enormous wealth one of the first things  that people did with their new wealth  whether they were very wealthy or  middling wealthy was to purchase homes  and therefore one of the key things  that's changed is that marriage now in  many cases depends on owning a home and  purchasing a home and the centrality of  that home ownership now occupies a  centrality in the spouse relationship  that was absent totally absent when it  was a public health of public housing  regime as it wasn't or socialism so the  first change is this housing piece and  their affluence piece the second is the  one-child policy which also has passed  in the same period 1980 is when it  starts to take effect and as soon as you  the state mandates one child per couple  you have radically changed the character  of marriage if marriage was about  becoming parents of usually two or more  children the sexual relationship with  spouses is different and also the  balance of power you could almost say in  the household is different so we're now  looking in urban China which is the  majority of people now in families in  which there are two adults and one child  and credly that creates a different  dynamic in a marriage or in which  children outnumber parents that's the  second thing the third is this vast  migration that we all know about 250  million Chinese have left their home  towns and moved elsewhere and this also  has changed marriage in a fundamental  way in that people find their partners  usually without very much supervision  from their parents direct supervision if  they're on the move and then it also  means that if there's a bad  ship a bad marriage people are much  freer to leave and start again so it's a  another huge change because in the  socialist era people were really nailed  to place and that was you were stuck  with the neighbors you had as well as  t